description:
crate-jmap-sharing-server/src/lib.rs:362-383 inside test_support::MockBackend:

    async fn destroy_object<O: SetObject + Send + Sync>(...) -> ... {
        let mut guard = self.state.lock().unwrap();
        if let Some(acct) = guard.get_mut(account_id.as_ref()) {
            if acct.notifications.remove(id).is_some() {
                acct.notification_state += 1;
                return Ok(());
            }
            if acct.principals.remove(id).is_some() {
                acct.principal_state += 1;
                return Ok(());
            }
        }
        Err(BackendSetError::SetError(SetError::new(SetErrorType::NotFound)))
    }

The function ignores the O type parameter and tries both maps. This is the shape you write when you've decided 'I'll figure out the type later' — but every call to destroy_object has a concrete O::TYPE_NAME at compile time. The function should dispatch on O::TYPE_NAME (compile-time-known constant string).

Suggested rewrite:
    match O::TYPE_NAME {
        "Principal" => {
            if acct.principals.remove(id).is_some() {
                acct.principal_state += 1;
                Ok(())
            } else { ... NotFound ... }
        }
        "ShareNotification" => {
            if acct.notifications.remove(id).is_some() {
                acct.notification_state += 1;
                Ok(())
            } else { ... NotFound ... }
        }
        other => Err(BackendSetError::Other(MockError(format!("unsupported type: {other}"))))
    }

This is mock code and not on a hot path, but the current shape is the textbook 'try every possibility' anti-pattern that obscures intent. A reader looking for 'how does the mock destroy Principal vs ShareNotification' has to read the whole function to know there's no distinction.
